# LSEEK(2)

## NAME

lseek, tell - move read/write pointer

## SYNOPSIS

```c
long lseek(int fildes, long offset, int whence);

long tell(int fildes);
```

## DESCRIPTION

The file descriptor refers to a file open for reading or writing. The read (resp. write)
pointer for the file is set as follows:

> If *whence* is 0, the pointer is set to *offset* bytes.

> If *whence* is 1, the pointer is set to its current location plus *offset*.

> If *whence* is 2, the pointer is set to the size of the file plus *offset*.

The returned value is the resulting pointer location.

The obsolete function *tell*(*fildes*) is identical to *lseek*(*fildes, 0L, 1*).

Seeking far beyond the end of a file, then writing, creates a gap or `hole`, which occupies no
physical space and reads as zeros.

## SEE ALSO

open(2), creat(2), fseek(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

-1 is returned for an undefined file descriptor, seek on a pipe, or seek to a position before
the beginning of file.

## BUGS

*Lseek* is a no-op on character special files.

## BESM-6 NOTES

**An offset is one word, and the gate lost an argument because of it.**

**off_t** is **int** is **long**, all one 48-bit word, so v7's two-word offset collapses to
one. The kernel's *sysent* entry said the call takes four arguments - a PDP-11 leftover where
the offset was a two-word **long** - and it says three now, matching the argument struct the
kernel's own *seek*() reads.

**That count is the contract nothing else can express.** A stub carries no arity; the kernel
reads it from *sysent* and b6sim from its own table, and every caller's prototype must agree
with both. A count that disagrees reads the arguments from the wrong slots *and* drifts the
caller's stack by a word per call. See intro(2).

*Tell* is declared by no header - v7 had none - so a caller declares it itself. Stime(2) shed
an argument in exactly the same way, its **time_t** having been two PDP-11 words.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_seek** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
